<body>

<h1>SSO Client Login API</h1>
<p>Single sign&#8209;on endpoint for a trusted external API client. The client authenticates itself
with a <code>client_id</code> / <code>client_secret</code> pair issued to a company in
<code>worksman_company_list</code>, and asserts the identity of one of its employees. The patient
account is created on the first call and logged in on every call, so the employee never needs a
password on the patient portal.</p>

<ul>
<li><b>Method &amp; path:</b> <code>POST /api/auth/sso-client-login</code></li>
<li><b>Authentication:</b> none (public route) &#8212; the caller is authenticated by <code>client_id</code> + <code>client_secret</code> in the body</li>
<li><b>Payload encryption:</b> not used &#8212; request and response are plain JSON</li>
<li><b>Controller:</b> <a href="controllers/authController.js">AuthController.ssoClientLogin</a></li>
<li><b>Route:</b> <a href="routes/authRoutes.js">routes/authRoutes.js</a></li>
<li><b>Validation:</b> <code>ssoClientLoginSchema</code> in <a href="validation/authValidation.js">validation/authValidation.js</a></li>
</ul>

<hr/>

<h2>Table of contents</h2>
<ul>
<li><a href="#how-it-differs">How it differs from /ssologin</a></li>
<li><a href="#identifiers">Client identifiers</a></li>
<li><a href="#request">Request</a></li>
<li><a href="#flow">Validation flow</a></li>
<li><a href="#ip-whitelist">IP whitelist rules</a></li>
<li><a href="#response">Success response</a></li>
<li><a href="#errors">Error responses</a></li>
<li><a href="#side-effects">What happens on first login</a></li>
<li><a href="#examples">Examples</a></li>
<li><a href="#onboarding">Onboarding a new client</a></li>
<li><a href="#security">Security notes</a></li>
</ul>

<hr/>

<h2><a name="how-it-differs"></a>How it differs from <code>/ssologin</code></h2>
<p>The existing <code>POST /api/auth/ssologin</code> trusts the caller completely: it takes company
names and employee details and creates whatever it is given. This endpoint keeps the same
outcome &#8212; a logged&#8209;in patient &#8212; but authenticates the calling system first.</p>

<table border="1">
<tr><th>&nbsp;</th><th>/ssologin</th><th>/sso-client-login</th></tr>
<tr><td>Caller identity</td><td>Not verified</td><td><code>client_id</code> + <code>client_secret</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>API entitlement</td><td>Not checked</td><td><code>api_access</code> must be <code>1</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>IP restriction</td><td>Not checked</td><td><code>api_ip_restriction</code> + <code>api_ip_whitelist</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>Parent company</td><td>Created from a company name</td><td>Resolved from <code>parent_client_id</code>, credentials checked on the parent</td></tr>
<tr><td>Company created on demand</td><td>Yes</td><td>No &#8212; the company must already exist and be active</td></tr>
<tr><td>Payload</td><td><code>origin_company</code>, <code>company_name</code>, <code>employee_*</code></td><td><code>client_id</code>, <code>client_secret</code>, <code>email</code>, <code>firstname</code>, &#8230;</td></tr>
</table>

<hr/>

<h2><a name="identifiers"></a>Client identifiers</h2>
<p>Both <code>client_id</code> and <code>parent_client_id</code> are the <code>employer_id</code> value
of a row in the <code>worksman_company_list</code> table. Only rows with <code>active = 1</code> are
considered.</p>

<table border="1">
<tr><th>Column</th><th>Type</th><th>Used for</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>employer_id</code></td><td>varchar(255)</td><td>The client id handed to the integrating system.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>parent_id</code></td><td>varchar(55)</td><td>The <code>id</code> of the parent company. <code>0</code> means the company has no parent.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>client_secret</code></td><td>text</td><td>The shared secret, compared in constant time.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>api_access</code></td><td>tinyint(1)</td><td>Must be <code>1</code> for the company to use the API.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>api_ip_restriction</code></td><td>tinyint(1)</td><td>When <code>1</code>, the caller IP must appear in the whitelist.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>api_ip_whitelist</code></td><td>text</td><td>The allowed caller IP addresses.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>active</code></td><td>tinyint(1)</td><td>Must be <code>1</code>; inactive companies are treated as unknown.</td></tr>
</table>

<blockquote>A parent company holds the credentials for its whole group. Child companies do not need
their own <code>client_secret</code> or <code>api_access</code> as long as the request is made with the
parent's <code>parent_client_id</code>.</blockquote>

<hr/>

<h2><a name="request"></a>Request</h2>

<h3>Headers</h3>
<table border="1">
<tr><th>Header</th><th>Value</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>Content-Type</code></td><td><code>application/json</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>X-Forwarded-For</code></td><td>Set by the proxy. Used to resolve the caller IP for the whitelist check; the first address in the list wins.</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Body</h3>
<table border="1">
<tr><th>Field</th><th>Type</th><th>Required</th><th>Description</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>parent_client_id</code></td><td>string</td><td>No</td><td>The <code>employer_id</code> of the parent company. When present, the credentials are validated against this company.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>client_id</code></td><td>string</td><td>Yes</td><td>The <code>employer_id</code> of the company the employee belongs to.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>client_secret</code></td><td>string</td><td>Yes</td><td>The secret of the parent company when <code>parent_client_id</code> is sent, otherwise the secret of <code>client_id</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>email</code></td><td>string</td><td>Yes</td><td>Employee email. This is the patient identity &#8212; it is the key used to find or create the account.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>mobile</code></td><td>string</td><td>No</td><td>Employee mobile number, stored on the new patient record.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>firstname</code></td><td>string</td><td>Yes*</td><td>Employee first name. <code>first_name</code> is accepted as an alias.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>last_name</code></td><td>string</td><td>No</td><td>Employee last name. <code>lastname</code> and <code>last-name</code> are accepted as aliases.</td></tr>
</table>
<p>* At least one of <code>firstname</code> / <code>first_name</code> must be present. Any field not
listed above is rejected by the schema with a 400.</p>

<hr/>

<h2><a name="flow"></a>Validation flow</h2>

<h4>1. Resolve the credential holder</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>With <code>parent_client_id</code></b> &#8212; the parent company is loaded by
<code>employer_id = parent_client_id</code>. If no active company matches, the request is rejected
with <b>401 Invalid parent client id.</b></li>
<li><b>Without <code>parent_client_id</code></b> &#8212; the company is loaded by
<code>employer_id = client_id</code>. If no active company matches, the request is rejected with
<b>401 Invalid client id.</b></li>
</ul>

<h4>2. Verify the credentials on that company</h4>
<ul>
<li><code>client_secret</code> must equal the stored <code>client_secret</code> &#8212;
otherwise <b>401 Invalid client secret.</b></li>
<li><code>api_access</code> must be <code>1</code> &#8212; otherwise
<b>403 API access is not enabled for this client.</b></li>
<li>If <code>api_ip_restriction</code> is <code>1</code>, the caller IP must be listed in
<code>api_ip_whitelist</code> &#8212; otherwise
<b>403 Your IP address is not whitelisted for this client.</b></li>
</ul>

<h4>3. Resolve <code>client_id</code> (only when <code>parent_client_id</code> was sent)</h4>
<ul>
<li>If <code>client_id</code> equals <code>parent_client_id</code>, the parent company itself is used.</li>
<li>Otherwise the child company is loaded by <code>employer_id = client_id</code>
<b>and</b> <code>parent_id</code> = the parent company's <code>id</code>. A company that exists but sits
under a different parent is rejected with
<b>401 The client id does not belong to the given parent client id.</b></li>
</ul>
<p>The resolved company &#8212; the child when there is one, otherwise the parent &#8212; is the company
the patient is attached to.</p>

<h4>4. Find or create the patient</h4>
<ul>
<li>The patient is looked up by <code>email</code>.</li>
<li>If the account exists but belongs to a different company, the request is rejected with
<b>403 This user belongs to another client.</b></li>
<li>If no account exists, one is created against the resolved company.</li>
<li>A JWT is issued and returned together with the patient profile.</li>
</ul>

<hr/>

<h2><a name="ip-whitelist"></a>IP whitelist rules</h2>
<ul>
<li>The check runs only when the company's <code>api_ip_restriction</code> is <code>1</code>.</li>
<li>The caller IP is read from <code>X-Forwarded-For</code> (first entry), then <code>X-Real-IP</code>,
then the socket address.</li>
<li>IPv4&#8209;mapped IPv6 addresses are normalised (<code>::ffff:203.0.113.5</code> is compared as
<code>203.0.113.5</code>) and <code>::1</code> is compared as <code>127.0.0.1</code>.</li>
<li>Entries in <code>api_ip_whitelist</code> may be separated by comma, semicolon, space or new line.</li>
<li>A single entry of <code>*</code> allows any IP.</li>
<li>An empty whitelist combined with <code>api_ip_restriction = 1</code> blocks every caller.</li>
</ul>

<hr/>

<h2><a name="response"></a>Success response</h2>
<p><b>200 OK</b> &#8212; plain JSON, not encrypted.</p>
<pre>{
  "success": true,
  "message": "User logged in successfully.",
  "data": true,
  "token": "eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9....",
  "userDetails": [
    {
      "loggedPatientId": 213,
      "uniquePatientId": "992269",
      "first_name": "John",
      "last_name": "Doe",
      "email": "john@example.com",
      "phone": "9876543210",
      "uuid": "6f1c0e2a-6b7d-4e3f-9a11-2c8d5f0b7e44",
      "role": "Patient",
      "isFirstLogin": 1,
      "isProfileCompleted": 0,
      "companyId": 16,
      "company_name": "Acme Inc"
    }
  ]
}</pre>
<p>The <code>token</code> is the standard patient JWT (payload <code>id</code>, <code>email</code>,
<code>role: "patient"</code>, valid for 1 day). Send it as <code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;</code>
on any other patient endpoint.</p>

<hr/>

<h2><a name="errors"></a>Error responses</h2>
<p>All errors use the plain (unencrypted) error envelope:</p>
<pre>{ "success": false, "message": "&lt;message&gt;", "errors": [] }</pre>

<table border="1">
<tr><th>HTTP</th><th>Message</th><th>Cause</th></tr>
<tr><td>400</td><td>Joi messages in <code>errors</code>, e.g. <code>"client_secret" is required</code></td><td>Missing, malformed or unknown field in the payload.</td></tr>
<tr><td>401</td><td>Invalid parent client id.</td><td><code>parent_client_id</code> does not match an active company.</td></tr>
<tr><td>401</td><td>Invalid client id.</td><td><code>client_id</code> does not match an active company (request sent without a parent).</td></tr>
<tr><td>401</td><td>Invalid client secret.</td><td><code>client_secret</code> does not match the stored secret, or no secret is configured.</td></tr>
<tr><td>401</td><td>The client id does not belong to the given parent client id.</td><td><code>client_id</code> is not the parent and is not one of its child companies.</td></tr>
<tr><td>403</td><td>API access is not enabled for this client.</td><td>The company's <code>api_access</code> is not <code>1</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>403</td><td>Your IP address is not whitelisted for this client.</td><td><code>api_ip_restriction</code> is <code>1</code> and the caller IP is not in <code>api_ip_whitelist</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>403</td><td>This user belongs to another client.</td><td>The email already exists under a different company.</td></tr>
<tr><td>400</td><td>Unable to create the user.</td><td>The patient record could not be inserted.</td></tr>
<tr><td>404</td><td>No patient found with the provided email.</td><td>The account could not be read back after being created or matched.</td></tr>
</table>

<blockquote>The 401 responses deliberately do not distinguish between "no such client" and "wrong
secret" beyond the message text, and the secret comparison runs in constant time so the response
time does not leak the stored value.</blockquote>

<hr/>

<h2><a name="side-effects"></a>What happens on first login</h2>
<p>When the email is not yet known, the endpoint creates the account before issuing the token:</p>
<ol>
<li>A row is inserted into <code>connectedCompaniesPatient</code> linking the email to the resolved
company (<code>connectionType = 1</code>, <code>status = 1</code>, <code>isActive = 1</code>) with a
generated <code>uniquePatientId</code>.</li>
<li>A row is inserted into <code>patient</code> with a generated <code>uuid</code>, the name, email,
mobile, <code>role = 'Patient'</code>, and <code>companyId</code> / <code>employer_id</code> set to the
resolved company.</li>
<li>The <code>connectedCompaniesPatient</code> row is updated with the new <code>patientId</code>.</li>
<li>Every doctor mapped to that company is assigned to the patient in
<code>patientCareTeam</code>, the same way email&#8209;verified signup does.</li>
</ol>
<p>A random password hash is stored because the external client owns the authentication &#8212; the
account cannot be used to log in through <code>POST /api/auth/login</code>. Subsequent calls with the
same email skip all of the above and only issue a fresh token.</p>

<hr/>

<h2><a name="examples"></a>Examples</h2>

<h3>Child company under a parent</h3>
<pre>POST /api/auth/sso-client-login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "parent_client_id": "513740",
  "client_id": "593402",
  "client_secret": "&lt;parent client secret&gt;",
  "email": "john@example.com",
  "mobile": "9876543210",
  "firstname": "John",
  "last_name": "Doe"
}</pre>
<p>Credentials are checked on company <code>513740</code>; the patient is attached to company
<code>593402</code>.</p>

<h3>Single company, no parent</h3>
<pre>POST /api/auth/sso-client-login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "client_id": "513740",
  "client_secret": "&lt;client secret&gt;",
  "email": "jane@example.com",
  "mobile": "9876543211",
  "firstname": "Jane",
  "last_name": "Roe"
}</pre>
<p>Credentials are checked on company <code>513740</code>, which is also the company the patient is
attached to.</p>

<h3>curl</h3>
<pre>curl -X POST https://&lt;host&gt;/api/auth/sso-client-login \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"parent_client_id":"513740","client_id":"593402","client_secret":"&lt;secret&gt;","email":"john@example.com","mobile":"9876543210","firstname":"John","last_name":"Doe"}'</pre>

<hr/>

<h2><a name="onboarding"></a>Onboarding a new client</h2>
<ol>
<li>Confirm the company exists in <code>worksman_company_list</code> with <code>active = 1</code> and a
non&#8209;empty <code>employer_id</code> &#8212; that value is the <code>client_id</code>.</li>
<li>Generate a long random <code>client_secret</code> and store it on the company row. For a group,
store it on the parent company only.</li>
<li>Set <code>api_access = 1</code> on that company.</li>
<li>To restrict by IP, set <code>api_ip_restriction = 1</code> and list the client's outbound
addresses in <code>api_ip_whitelist</code>.</li>
<li>For a group, make sure each child company's <code>parent_id</code> holds the parent's
<code>id</code> (not its <code>employer_id</code>).</li>
<li>Hand the client its <code>client_id</code>, <code>parent_client_id</code> where applicable, and the
secret over a secure channel.</li>
</ol>

<hr/>

<h2><a name="security"></a>Security notes</h2>
<ul>
<li>The secret travels in the request body, so the endpoint must only ever be served over HTTPS.</li>
<li>The endpoint creates portal accounts on demand. Only issue credentials to systems that already
authenticate the employee on their side &#8212; anyone holding a valid secret can obtain a token for
any email under their own company.</li>
<li>An email that already exists under a different company is rejected rather than transferred, so a
client cannot take over another company's patient.</li>
<li>Rotate <code>client_secret</code> by updating the company row; the change takes effect on the next
request with no restart.</li>
<li>Set <code>api_access = 0</code> to switch a client off immediately.</li>
<li>The route sits under the global <code>/api</code> rate limiter.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>
